# Copy this file to .env and uncomment what you want to change.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every value below is the built-in default, so an untouched copy changes nothing.
# Variables already set in the real environment win over this file — a value passed
# to docker compose, or set for a single run, is not overridden by a stale .env.
#
# Docker reads this file too, but on the host: compose substitutes ${VAR} into its
# own environment block. The file is deliberately kept out of the image.

# --- Serving -----------------------------------------------------------------

# PORT=8787
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# Origins allowed to call the API. Only relevant if you serve the web app from a
# different origin than the API; the single-container deployment does not.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:4173

# trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Access ------------------------------------------------------------------

# none | password | multiuser  (multiuser is not implemented and refuses to start)
# AUTH_MODE=none

# Required when AUTH_MODE=password. No default, deliberately.
# AUTH_PASSWORD=

# --- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------

# One SQLite file. Backing up means copying it.
# DATABASE_PATH=./data/dwc.db

# --- Measurement -------------------------------------------------------------

# Explicit resolvers, never the host's. A local resolver that refuses queries
# would otherwise make every measurement a property of your machine.
# DNS_RESOLVERS=1.1.1.1,8.8.8.8,9.9.9.9

# Requests per run used to judge consistency. Below 5, variance is noise and the
# engine refuses to draw a conclusion from it.
# STABILITY_SAMPLES=5

# Where the browser measures its latency baseline.
#
# Unset means same-origin. On a local install that baseline is loopback, which
# says nothing about anyone's internet — so "your connection" and "the path
# between" correctly report as not measured rather than inventing a number.
#
# Any URL your browser can reach over the internet will do. The round trip is
# timed without reading the response, so the far end grants nothing and does not
# need to know this tool exists. A local address is still refused.
#
#   CONTROL_URL=https://www.google.com/generate_204
#
# That endpoint returns an empty 204 and exists for connectivity checks, so it is
# cheap to call — but it is a third party, and nothing here contacts one unless
# you uncomment this line.
#
# How far it gets you depends on what you point it at:
#
#   any endpoint       "your connection" becomes measurable
#   another instance   "the path between" becomes measurable as well
#
# The route needs a second instance because judging it means subtracting your
# link's cost from your time to the site, and that subtraction assumes the two
# are comparable measurements. A large provider answers from whichever edge is
# nearest you, so the baseline comes out short by however far the site actually
# is — and the leftover, which the report would blame on your provider, is really
# just distance.
#
# A paired instance needs your origin in its CORS_ORIGINS. This server never
# fetches the URL; only your browser does.
# CONTROL_URL=

# --- Limits ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Diagnostics per client IP per minute. This is the one that matters: a probe
# opens connections to a third party on a stranger's behalf.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=20

# Everything else per client IP per minute. A runaway-client backstop, not a
# throttle on reading — page loads and report views spend from this.
# READS_PER_MINUTE=600

# MAX_REDIRECTS=10
# MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=5000000

# --- Timeouts (milliseconds) -------------------------------------------------

# TIMEOUT_DNS_MS=5000
# TIMEOUT_CONNECT_MS=8000
# TIMEOUT_TLS_MS=8000
# TIMEOUT_HTTP_MS=15000
# TIMEOUT_TOTAL_MS=45000

# --- Development only --------------------------------------------------------

# Where the Vite dev server proxies /api. Read by apps/web/vite.config.ts at
# startup, not by the API, and irrelevant to the built app or the container.
# API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
